# Crypto invariants

These are not style preferences. Each one, if broken, either makes existing data
permanently unreadable or silently removes a guarantee the system claims to
provide. Decisions and reasoning: `doc/adr/0005-kodium-readiness-gate.md`,
`doc_add/ТЕХДОКУМЕНТАЦИЯ/03-криптография.md`.

## Which library provides what

- **NaCl layer** — SecretBox, Box, Ed25519, HKDF — **only Kodium**. Do not add a
  second library for these and do not write the primitives yourself.
- **ML-KEM-768** — `asia.hombre:kyber` (KyberKotlin), behind the single wrapper
  `messenger-crypto/.../Mlkem768.kt`. Kodium's own ML-KEM is broken and must not
  be called: it truncates SHAKE output, filling the matrix `Â` with 3% entropy.
- **BouncyCastle** — `testImplementation` only. It is the independent oracle, not
  a product dependency. Removing it from the test scope removes the only thing
  that would catch a repeat of the Kodium defect.

Changing a provider means changing exactly one wrapper file. If a change needs
edits in `EscrowModule`, `PersonalMessageSealer` or `GroupKeyManager`, the
wrapper boundary is being broken — stop and reconsider.

## Never change these derivations

Each one is baked into data that already exists. Changing it does not migrate
anything; it makes the old data unreadable and the old keys unrecognisable.

| What | Where | What breaks |
|---|---|---|
| `KodiumPrivateKey.fromRaw(seed)` semantics | device and identity keys | **every device's keys change** — an account migration, not a refactor |
| `AccountMnemonic` wordlist and index order | 12-word phrases | already-issued phrases stop resolving |
| HKDF labels `tima/escrow/v1`, `tima/account-identity/v1`, `tima/account-backup/v1` | escrow, identity, backup | existing escrow blobs and backups unreadable |
| HKDF label `tima/local-store/v1` | `LocalStoreCipher` — at-rest key of the local database | **every local conversation becomes unreadable** on every device: the key is derived, not stored, so there is nothing to migrate from |
| `chunk:<i>` derivation | media chunks | stored media unreadable |
| Canonical byte layout | envelope signatures | every stored signature fails verification |

If one of these genuinely must change, it needs a version field and a migration
path, decided before the first line of code.

## Two implementations must agree byte for byte

Some byte layouts exist twice, in Go and in Kotlin. They are normative, and a
one-byte difference produces a signature nobody can verify — with no error
message pointing at the cause.

- canonical envelope bytes: `server/internal/crypto/canonical.go` ↔
  `messenger-crypto/.../CanonicalBytes.kt`
- device-link signing bytes: `linkSigningBytes` ↔ `DeviceLinkSignature.signingBytes`

Change one side, change the other in the same commit, and prove it with a vector.

## Test vectors

`schema/test-vectors/vectors.json` is generated by external implementations
(`tweetnacl-js`, `@noble/post-quantum`). It is the canary.

- A failing vector means **our code is wrong**, not that the vector is stale.
  Editing a vector to make a test pass destroys the only external check we have.
- Changing a vector is changing the protocol. It needs the same deliberation as
  changing a wire format.
- `VectorsTest` and `CrossImplementationTest` are complementary: the first pins
  one input per primitive and catches systematic errors, the second runs many
  random inputs and catches input-dependent ones. Neither replaces the other.
- A canary only checks what is distinguishable inside it. `mlkem768_escrow` uses
  a seed of 64 identical bytes, so it cannot detect a swapped `d`/`z` order —
  that had to be found by a separate cross-check.

## Secrets

Never log, print, or put into a summary: message keys, group keys, media keys,
device or identity private keys, escrow material, mnemonics, tokens, one-time
codes, QR payloads, presigned URLs. This includes error messages and test output.

## Escrow

`escrow_mlkem_ct` and `escrow_wrapped_key` are `NOT NULL`: escrow is mandatory on
every message, not a feature flag. Code paths that would produce a message
without escrow are a design error, not an optimisation.

The key commitment is computed **once**, from the same key used to encrypt. Every
delivery path must lead to exactly that key — that is the whole point of it.
